+ashtadhyayi | 6th century BCE..4th century BCE | India | language, science | all | Aṣṭādhyāyī | Panini's grammar | The Ashtadhyayi is a grammar text that describes a form of the Sanskrit language. It is attributed to Panini, and its composition is dated to somewhere between the 6th and 4th centuries BCE. | About 2,500 years ago a scholar named Panini wrote down the rules of the Sanskrit language in a famous book.
+tolkappiyam | 2nd century BCE | Tamil Nadu | language, arts | south | Tolkāppiyam | The Tolkappiyam | The Tolkappiyam is the oldest extant Tamil grammar and the oldest extant long work of Tamil literature. It is difficult to date: one scholar places its earliest layer between the 2nd and 1st century BCE, another dates it no earlier than the 2nd century CE. | The Tolkappiyam is the oldest book of Tamil grammar that still exists. Nobody is sure how old it is: its first parts may be about 2,000 years old.
+natya-shastra | 200 BCE..200 | India | arts, language | all | Natya Shastra | The Natya Shastra | The Natya Shastra is a Sanskrit treatise on the performing arts, attributed to the sage Bharata. Its first complete compilation is dated to between 200 BCE and 200 CE, though estimates vary. | The Natya Shastra is a very old Sanskrit book that explains how to act, dance and make music.
+arthashastra | 1st century | India | power, trade, war | all | Arthashastra | The Arthashastra is compiled | The Arthashastra is an ancient Sanskrit treatise on statecraft, politics, economic policy and military strategy. It is likely the work of several authors over centuries, compiled into a new treatise in the 1st century CE. | The Arthashastra is an ancient Indian book about how to run a kingdom: its money, its army and its laws.
+kural | 300 BCE..500 | Tamil Nadu | language, arts, daily | south | Kural | The Tirukkural | The Tirukkural, or Kural, is a classic Tamil text on everyday morality made of 1,330 short couplets of seven words each. It has been dated variously from 300 BCE to the 5th century CE. | The Kural is a famous Tamil book of 1,330 tiny two-line poems about how to live a good life.
+shakuntala | 4th century | India | arts, language | all | Shakuntala (play) | Kalidasa's Shakuntala | Abhijnanashakuntalam, The Recognition of Shakuntala, is a Sanskrit play by the poet Kalidasa. Its exact date is uncertain, but Kalidasa is often placed in the 4th century CE. | The poet Kalidasa wrote a famous play in Sanskrit about a girl named Shakuntala. He probably lived more than 1,600 years ago.
+halmidi | 450..500 | Halmidi | language | south | Halmidi inscription | The oldest Kannada inscription | The Halmidi inscription, found in a village of the Hassan district of Karnataka, is the oldest known inscription in the Kannada language. It is often dated to between 450 and 500 CE. | A stone found at the village of Halmidi carries the oldest writing we know in the Kannada language. It is about 1,500 years old.
+cilappatikaram | 5th century | Tamil Nadu | arts, language, women | south | Cilappatikaram | The first Tamil epic | The Cilappatikaram, a poem of 5,730 lines, is the earliest Tamil epic. It tells a tragic love story of ordinary people, is attributed to the prince turned Jain monk Ilango Adigal, and was probably composed in the 5th century CE. | The Cilappatikaram is the oldest long story-poem in Tamil. It is a sad love story about ordinary people, not kings.
+nataraja | 6th century | Badami cave temples | arts, religion | deccan | Nataraja; Badami cave temples | Shiva as the cosmic dancer | Nataraja is a depiction of Shiva as the divine cosmic dancer. Stone reliefs at the Ellora Caves and the Badami Caves, made by around the 6th century, are among the oldest Nataraja sculptures in India. | Nataraja is the god Shiva shown as a dancer. Some of the oldest carvings of him, about 1,500 years old, are in the caves at Badami.
+rajatarangini | 12th century | Kashmir Valley | language, power | himalaya | Rajatarangini | A chronicle of Kashmir's kings | The Rajatarangini is a chronicle in verse, part legend and part history, of the kings of Kashmir. It was written in Sanskrit by the Kashmiri historian Kalhana in the 12th century CE. | In the 1100s a writer named Kalhana wrote a long poem telling the story of the kings of Kashmir.
+hamzanama | 1562 | India | arts | all | Hamzanama | Akbar's Hamzanama | The Hamzanama tells the legendary exploits of Hamza, an uncle of Muhammad. Its best-known copy is the enormous illustrated manuscript commissioned by the Mughal emperor Akbar about 1562. | Around 1562 the emperor Akbar ordered a giant illustrated book of adventure stories.
+ramcharitmanas | 16th century | Awadh | religion, language, arts | gangetic | Ramcharitmanas | The Ramcharitmanas | The Ramcharitmanas is an epic poem in the Awadhi language, composed by the 16th-century bhakti poet Tulsidas. Its chief inspiration is the Ramayana of Valmiki. | In the 1500s the poet Tulsidas retold the story of Rama in a long poem in the Awadhi language.
+ain-i-akbari | 1589..1596 | India | power, trade, language | all | Ain-i-Akbari | The Ain-i-Akbari | The Ain-i-Akbari, the "Administration of Akbar", is a detailed account of how the Mughal Empire was run under the emperor Akbar. His court historian Abu'l Fazl wrote it in Persian between 1589 and 1596. | The emperor Akbar's historian wrote a book describing exactly how the Mughal Empire was run.
+guru-granth-sahib | 1604-08-29 | Golden Temple | religion, language | northwest | Guru Granth Sahib | The Sikh scripture is completed | The Guru Granth Sahib is the central scripture of Sikhism, regarded by Sikhs as their final and eternal Guru. Its compilation was completed on 29 August 1604, and it was first installed in the Golden Temple at Amritsar on 1 September 1604. | The holy book of the Sikhs was finished in 1604 and placed in the Golden Temple at Amritsar.
+hickys-gazette | 1780..1782 | Kolkata | language, power | east | Hicky's Bengal Gazette | Asia's first printed newspaper | Hicky's Bengal Gazette, an English-language weekly published in Calcutta, was the first newspaper printed in Asia. It ran from 1780 to 1782, until the East India Company seized its types and press. | The first printed newspaper in Asia came out in Calcutta in 1780. Two years later the British company shut it down.
